ors to our community , \" Smith <37 added> . \"We <22 take> our <40 stature> in the community very <41 seriously> . Through the Chargers and the NFL , our players <23 receive> one of the most comprehensive education and support programs in <3 sports> to help them <25 deal with> issues that <26 affect> all segments of society . \" Smith didn't <85 return> a <24 call> <27 seeking> <12 further comment> . Chargers receiver Vincent Jackson was <31 arrested> for <32 investigation> of <4 DUI> on Jan . 6 , five days before San Diego's playoff <5 loss> at Pittsburgh . Jackson is <42 due> in court Feb . 17 . He was <43 on probation> for a <30 previous DUI arrest> , the CHP <38 said> . Williams , an 11 - year veteran , <29 shared> the team's Most Valuable Player Award with quarterback Philip Rivers this season . The Chargers reached the playoffs with an 8 - 8 record . Coincidentally , the Chargers were the first NFL team to implement Safe Ride Solutions , a program in which pro athletes who've had too much to drink can call a toll - free number and request a ride from an off - duty police officer . For implementing the program , the Chargers were awarded the NFL's Winston - Shell Award for demonstrating commitment and dedication to developing unique and innovative ideas and solutions . Smith often speaks of signing \"character\" players , yet the Chargers have had their share of off - field problems in recent seasons . In 2006 , there were seven run - ins with the law by Chargers players , including linebacker Steve Foley's shooting by an off - duty Coronado police officer who suspected him of drunken driving , and two other players who were arrested for investigation of DUI . Besides the arrests , linebacker Shawne Merriman was suspended four games for testing positive for steroids in 2006 , and linebacker Stephen Cooper was suspended by the NFL for the first four games of the 2008 season after testing positive for a banned stimulant .", "gold": [["9", "causes", "8"], ["31", 